('5370e8d2-2bc5-59fe-89ec-a39f13a202d3','e7d4e64a-095e-5557-b699-6855fb3029dc',1,'Peel the onions, cut them in half from top to bottom and finely slice into half-moon pieces. Put in a colander placed over a bowl and sprinkle with salt, lightly turning over the onion pieces with your hands so the surfaces are all covered. Set aside for an hour or so to brine.',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-03 08:00:00+00',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-03 08:00:00+00','themealdb-import','themealdb-import',0),
('3e0f29a9-efc5-51b1-b4e8-7be8e9bec9e5','e7d4e64a-095e-5557-b699-6855fb3029dc',2,'Meanwhile put the vinegar, 50ml/2fl oz water and the sugar in a saucepan. Bring to a simmer, stirring to help the sugar dissolve, and cook for a couple of minutes. Set aside.',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-03 08:00:00+00',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-03 08:00:00+00','themealdb-import','themealdb-import',0),
('86655c72-bf64-52d8-80cf-0d20a8b12e6b','e7d4e64a-095e-5557-b699-6855fb3029dc',3,'Pack the onions into the sterilised jars, sprinkling in a little pepper as you go. Cover with the warm vinegar and finish by tucking a couple of bay leaves down the side of the jars. Seal. The onions are best kept in the fridge and used within to 4 weeks.',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-03 08:00:00+00',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-03 08:00:00+00','themealdb-import','themealdb-import',0),
('cd76ecc5-cd8e-575e-b952-795c5e9d1f58','59a0ee4d-f916-5f80-adf9-b3c5ff9ce6ba',1,'In a small saucepan heat the butter, milk and salt until the butter is melted. Allow the mixture to cool until it is lukewarm.',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-03 08:00:00+00',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-03 08:00:00+00','themealdb-import','themealdb-import',0),
('e7e1a6b5-0f73-5956-b24d-897e0cb46596','59a0ee4d-f916-5f80-adf9-b3c5ff9ce6ba',2,'In a large bowl, stir together the flours, yeast, cardamom and sugar until combined.',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-03 08:00:00+00',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-03 08:00:00+00','themealdb-import','themealdb-import',0),
('d2677c38-ffa0-5662-a824-d22eea3b58fa','59a0ee4d-f916-5f80-adf9-b3c5ff9ce6ba',3,'Make a well in the centre and crack in the eggs. Pour in the lukewarm milk mixture and stir everything together to form a sticky dough. You may have to use your hands as the dough becomes stiffer.',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-03 08:00:00+00',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-03 08:00:00+00','themealdb-import','themealdb-import',0),
('b3df9474-f517-5a98-a994-0d05f5449c1f','59a0ee4d-f916-5f80-adf9-b3c5ff9ce6ba',4,'Oil the work surface with a teaspoon of olive oil. Turn the dough out onto the oiled surface and knead vigorously for 5–8 minutes, using a plastic scraper as needed to prise the dough from the work surface. Don’t be tempted to add flour, as this will make the buns dry and tough. Keep kneading until the dough is considerably less sticky, smoother and more elastic.',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-03 08:00:00+00',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-03 08:00:00+00','themealdb-import','themealdb-import',0),
('0406f99e-7886-5d14-93d3-26679b256f70','59a0ee4d-f916-5f80-adf9-b3c5ff9ce6ba',5,'Shape into a ball, and put into a large, greased bowl. Cover the bowl with a clean tea towel and set aside in a warm place to prove for an hour, or until doubled in size.',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-03 08:00:00+00',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-03 08:00:00+00','themealdb-import','themealdb-import',0),
